 Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Each submission names its base revision ID. When concurrent submissions arrive from different base states, both are retained as competing complete revisions. A coordinator reviews and selects which revision becomes current, or merges them manually. This approach preserves each worker's submitted visit as a coherent unit and avoids maintaining merge rules for scientifically related fields.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+Review workload is higher — even unrelated field edits create competing revisions — but the tradeoff is accepted: maintaining field-aware merge rules risks combining edits across scientific relationships the rules cannot see.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Submission Envelope
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+A submission carries:
+- Visit ID (client-generated UUID, stable across all edits to the visit)
+- Base revision ID (the revision from which this submission derives; null for the first submission)
+- Author ID (authenticated worker)
+- Idempotency key (prevents duplicate processing on connection retry)
+- Payload (the complete visit state: observation date, observer list, species cover, notes)
+- Timestamp of submission acceptance (server-assigned)
+
+### Current-State Representation
+
+A visit's current state is determined by its concurrency resolution:
+- **Resolved**: one selected revision is current; unresolved competing revisions are archived
+- **Unresolved**: two or more competing revisions exist from the same base; no current state is selected
+
+Retrieval returns:
+- The current revision (if resolved)
+- A list of competing revisions awaiting resolution (if unresolved)
+- Metadata linking each revision to its author, base revision ID, and server receipt time
+
+A visit is export-eligible only when resolved.
+
+### Resolution Operation
+
+A coordinator resolves competing revisions by:
+- Selecting one revision as current, archiving others
+- Or manually merging revisions by submitting a new revision that cites all competing predecessors
+
+The resolution operation records:
+- Coordinator identity
+- Contributing submission IDs (the revisions involved)
+- The selected or manually-authored resolution outcome
+- Resolution timestamp
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync will retain base revision IDs in local drafts, display competing revisions when syncing, and retry submission only when the base revision is no longer valid. Review-export will present unresolved visits with side-by-side revision comparison and exclude them from the CSV export until resolved.
